Here is the commit message:
net: dsa: qca8k: check the correct variable in
 qca8k_set_mac_eee()

This code check "reg" but "ret" was intended so the error handling will
never trigger.

Fixes: 7c9896e37807 ("net: dsa: qca8k: check return value of read functions correctly")
Signed-off-by: Dan Carpenter <dan.carpenter@oracle.com>
Reviewed-by: Vladimir Oltean <olteanv@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: David S. Miller <davem@davemloft.net>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by checking the wrong variable (`reg`) for error conditions instead of the intended variable (`ret`), which holds the return value of the read function. This oversight meant that error handling would never trigger, leading to potential undetected failures in the operation of the function.

Here is the patch:

 static int
 qca8k_set_mac_eee(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, struct ethtool_eee *eee)
 {
 	struct qca8k_priv *priv = (struct qca8k_priv *)ds->priv;
 	u32 lpi_en = QCA8K_REG_EEE_CTRL_LPI_EN(port);
 	u32 reg;
 	int ret;

 	mutex_lock(&priv->reg_mutex);
 	ret = qca8k_read(priv, QCA8K_REG_EEE_CTRL, &reg);
-	if (reg < 0) {
-		ret = reg;
+	if (ret < 0)
 		goto exit;
-	}

 	if (eee->eee_enabled)
 		reg |= lpi_en;
 	else
 		reg &= ~lpi_en;
 	ret = qca8k_write(priv, QCA8K_REG_EEE_CTRL, reg);

 exit:
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->reg_mutex);
 	return ret;
 }



